From be5e42b3aaafa584a9fa4f41f3ac74d8dbe0e455 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 15:39:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(a11y): five real targets under the AA floor, and an audit
 that was inventing the rest
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The first production run of audit:rendered reported 86 tap-target failures, 59
unreachable routes and 2 unreachable scrollers. One of those three numbers was
about the site.

WHAT THE INSTRUMENT WAS GETTING WRONG

1. IT MEASURED THE CHECKBOX, NOT THE TARGET. The consent checkboxes came back
   13×13 and led the report as the worst thing on the site. They ARE 13×13 —
   and every one sits inside <label class="ef-consent">, so what a thumb hits is
   the label: full form width, three lines tall. WCAG 2.5.8 measures the target,
   and for a labelled control the label is part of it. The detector now walks to
   the enclosing label (or the label[for=…]) and measures that. Reporting the
   input alone invented a defect AND buried the real ones under it.

2. IT ROUNDED AFTER COMPARING. getBoundingClientRect returns fractions. A link
   laid out at exactly 24px comes back as 23.98…, fails a `>= 24` test, and is
   then printed as "24×24 — below the floor of 24×24". Six link styles on this
   site land on exactly 24, on every page, at three viewports. That is most of
   the 86, and it is the kind of output that teaches a person to stop believing
   the tool.

3. networkidle NEVER FIRES HERE. 12 routes unreachable on the first run, 59 on
   the second, on a site that answers every one of them with 200 to curl. That
   is the instrument. `networkidle` wants 500ms with at most two open
   connections; this site has a chat widget, an analytics beacon and a CSS
   marquee, so on a cold deployment it never goes quiet and every page "fails"
   after 45 seconds. Now: `load`, a 600ms settle, and one retry on
   domcontentloaded — because a single timeout on a cold function is not a fact
   about the page.

WHAT SURVIVED, AND IS FIXED

Five things were genuinely under 24×24 on a phone, and one table could not be
read without a finger:

  .tlx-crumbs a       18px  breadcrumb links, on every deep page
  .jc-link            22px  "Read what was measured" — the CTA on a proof card
  .ill-full           19px  "View full size" on every diagram
  photo credit        18px  the Unsplash photographer link, twice in
                            RotatingBackground. It is an obligation under the
                            licence, so it has to be reachable, not just present.
  .wp-table-wrap            /commercial's price table scrolls 278→307px on a
                            phone and held nothing focusable. Eight of the nine
                            wrappers in the app already carry role="region",
                            tabIndex={0} and a name. This one did not, so the
                            right-hand columns could only be reached by dragging.

inline-flex with min-height rather than a padding guess: each link keeps its own
text metrics and claims the height a thumb needs, and nothing above or below it
moves.

WHAT THIS RUN ALSO CONFIRMED, ON PRODUCTION

  horizontal-overflow: 0        was 62 — the utility strip fix holds
  access-control-allow-origin: https://ecowoods.ca on /, * on /llms.txt

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
